// Title: Dynamic .NET Compilation Via Csc.EXE - Hunting
// Sigma ID: acf2807c-805b-4042-aab9-f86b6ba9cb2b
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2023-08-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1027.004
// Tags: detection.threat-hunting
// Description: Detects execution of "csc.exe" to compile .NET code. Attackers often leverage this to compile
//   code on the fly and use it in other stages.
// False positives:
//   - Many legitimate applications make use of dynamic compilation. Use this rule to hunt for anomalies
// References:
//   - https://securityboulevard.com/2019/08/agent-tesla-evading-edr-by-removing-api-hooks/
//   - https://www.clearskysec.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/MuddyWater-Operations-in-Lebanon-and-Oman.pdf
//   - https://app.any.run/tasks/c6993447-d1d8-414e-b856-675325e5aa09/
//   - https://twitter.com/gN3mes1s/status/1206874118282448897
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules-threat-hunting/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_csc_compilation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\csc.exe" and ProcessCommandLine contains "/noconfig /fullpaths @"
